In C#, the static keyword is used to declare a class member (such as a method, property, or field) that belongs to the class itself rather than to instances of the class. When you declare a member as static, it is shared among all instances of the class and can be accessed without creating an instance of the class.

Here are some key points about static members in C#:

1. Static Fields: These are variables that belong to the class rather than to individual instances. They are initialized once and shared among all instances.

2. Static Methods: These are methods that belong to the class rather than to instances. They can't access instance members (fields or methods) directly because they don't operate on a specific instance.

3. Static Classes: A static class is a class that cannot be instantiated. It can only contain static members. It is useful for grouping related utility methods or constants.

4. Static Constructors: A static constructor is used to initialize static members of a class. It is called once when the class is first accessed, before any instance is created or any static method is called.

